{"id":"184efab4-937a-4b64-83d6-e445b3f43327","object":"chat.completion","created":1784808678,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    # validate\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be dict\\\")\\n    for key in ('text','passes','max_rounds'):\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"missing key {key}\\\")\\n    text =","reasoning_content":"We need to implement a function `solve(case)` that given a case with text, passes, max_rounds returns a JSON-serializable object. The task is about running a fixed-point pass scheduler. Each pass has a name, old, new (strings). The rule: each pass replaces the first occurrence of old with new in the current text, but only if it changes something. We perform sweeps in order of passes. Each pass does at most one rewrite per sweep (first eligible). Record passes that change the artifact. Continue sweeps until no changes or max_rounds reached. Return artifact, application trace, fixed-point flag.\n\nGiven the fixture, we need to parse passes list, then simulate. The text is a string. Each pass: find first occurrence of old in current text; if found, replace that occurrence with new (only the first). Then record that pass name in trace if changed. After each full sweep (i.e., after iterating all passes in order), if no changes in that sweep, stop (fixed point). Also stop if number of sweeps (rounds) reaches max_rounds. Return object with keys: \"artifact\" (final text), \"applied_passes\" (list of strings names in order applied), \"fixed_point\" (bool, whether fixed point reached (i.e., no changes in last sweep or no changes overall? Actually typical: fixed point if after a full sweep none changed and we stop. Also can be reached if max_rounds exhausted but still changes? The flag indicates whether we stopped because of fixed point (i.e., no changes in last sweep) or because of budget. So fixed_point should be True if the last sweep had zero changes and we stopped normally; False if we stopped because max_rounds reached but there were still changes in the last sweep. But need to confirm: \"fixed-point flag\" likely True if algorithm terminated because no changes occurred in a full sweep. \n\nImplementation: define solve(case). Validate that case has required fields: text (string), passes (list of dicts with name, old, new), max_rounds (int >=0). Then simulate.\n\nEdge cases: old may be empty? Probably not; but we assume non-empty. Replacement should be first occurrence only. Use str.replace(old, new, 1) to replace first occurrence. But careful: if old appears multiple times, we only replace first. str.replace with count=1 works.\n\nWe need to record applied_passes: list of pass names in order of application across all sweeps. Only if the pass actually changed the text.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- artifact = case['text']\n- applied = []\n- for round in range(max_rounds):\n    changed_this_sweep = False\n    for p in passes:\n        old = p['old']; new = p['new']\n        if old in artifact:\n            # replace first occurrence\n            artifact = artifact.replace(old, new, 1)\n            applied.append(p['name'])\n            changed_this_sweep = True\n    if not changed_this_sweep:\n        # fixed point reached\n        return {\"artifact\": artifact, \"applied_passes\": applied, \"fixed_point\": True}\n- after loop (max_rounds exhausted): return {\"artifact\": artifact, \"applied_passes\": applied, \"fixed_point\": False}\n\nBut note: what if multiple passes in same sweep? Each pass may change artifact, affecting subsequent passes in that sweep. That's fine.\n\nOne nuance: The problem statement says \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" That's exactly what we do: first occurrence.\n\nAlso \"Record only passes that change the artifact.\" So we record only when replacement actually happened (i.e., old existed). Our code does that.\n\nCheck fixtures: text \"aaaa bb cc\", passes: halve-a: \"aa\"->\"a\", drop-b: \"bb\"->\"b\", drop-c: \" cc\"->\"\". max_rounds=8.\n\nLet's simulate: round0: halve-a: find \"aa\" at positions? \"aaaa\" -> first \"aa\" from start? \"aa\" at index0? Replace first \"aa\" with \"a\" gives \"aa bb cc\"? Actually \"aaaa\" -> replace first \"aa\" -> \"aa bb cc\" (because first two 'a's become 'a', leaving \"aa\" from remaining). Then drop-b: \"bb\" found -> replace with \"b\" -> \"aa b cc\". Then drop-c: \" cc\" found -> replace with \"\" -> \"aa b\". End of sweep. changed=true.\nRound1: halve-a: now \"aa\" in \"aa b\"? Yes, first occurrence at start -> replace with \"a\" => \"a b\". drop-b: \"bb\"? not present, skip. drop-c: \" cc\"? not present. changed? Only halve-a changed -> record. changed_this_sweep=true. After sweep, artifact \"a b\".\nRound2: halve-a: \"aa\"? not present. None. changed_this_sweep=false -> fixed point True. So final artifact \"a b\", applied_passes: each round: halve-a, drop-b, drop-c from round0, then halve-a from round1. So list: [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\",\"halve-a\"]? Wait careful: We record per pass only if changed. In round0: halve-a changed, drop-b changed, drop-c changed -> recorded. In round1: halve-a changed. So total [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\",\"halve-a\"]. But note order: within round0 we process passes in order: halve-a, drop-b, drop-c. Then round1: halve-a. So that matches.\n\nBut need to ensure that we don't record a pass that didn't change. Our code only records when we perform replace. That's correct.\n\nNow output must be JSON-serializable object. Return a dict with keys \"artifact\", \"applied_passes\", \"fixed_point\". The example fixture doesn't show expected output, but we must produce exactly that.\n\nAlso need to validate input: raise ValueError if invalid. But the problem says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". We can check that case has required keys and types. For simplicity, we can check that 'text' is a string, 'passes' is a list of dicts each with 'name', 'old', 'new' strings, 'max_rounds' is int >=0. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso ensure we don't mutate case input. Accept the case dict, extract values.\n\nWe must not use any external libraries, only standard library.\n\nNow produce JSON string with implementation key. The implementation value is Python source code as a string (escaped). We need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The source code must contain `def solve(case):` and return the result.\n\nAlso note: The function must return a JSON-serializable object, so we return a dict.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The passes may have names that are not unique? Possibly but fine.\n\nMake sure we handle empty passes list? Then artifact stays same, no applied_passes, fixed_point true (since no change in first sweep). But max_rounds may be 0? If max_rounds=0, we should not do any sweeps. Then return artifact unchanged, applied_passes empty, fixed_point? Should we consider that fixed point? The algorithm would not have run any sweeps. The problem says \"Repeat full sweeps until none changes or the round budget is consumed.\" If budget is 0, we cannot perform any sweeps. So we should treat it as if we ran 0 rounds, so no changes, so fixed point? But careful: if max_rounds=0, we never check for changes. Typically fixed point is defined by termination condition after sweeps. If no sweeps performed, it's trivially fixed point? Might be ambiguous. However, the fixture has max_rounds=8, so we can assume positive. But to be safe, we can return fixed_point=True when no sweeps performed? Or False? I think it's reasonable to return True because no changes occurred. But the problem might expect False if budget exhausted? Since budget 0 means we stop immediately without checking. I'll assume max_rounds >=1 for given test cases. But we can decide: if max_rounds == 0, return artifact, empty list, fixed_point=True (no changes needed). We'll mention in code.\n\nBut the problem says \"Return the artifact, application trace, and fixed-point flag.\" So we return.\n\nNow produce the JSON.\n\nEnsure we escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nI'll write the source code as a multi-line string and then escape it.\n\nWe need to encode as JSON string. In Python, we can use json.dumps on the source string to get proper escaping.\n\nBut the final output must be JSON with one key implementation. So we'll build dict and json.dumps it.\n\nIn the response, we must output exactly that JSON string, no extra text.\n\nSo final answer: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and the source code as a string.\n\nLet's draft the source code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be dict\")\n    for key in ('text','passes','max_rounds'):\n        if key not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing key {key}\")\n    text = case['text']\n    if not isinstance(text, str):\n        raise ValueError(\"text must be string\")\n    passes = case['passes']\n    if not isinstance(passes, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"passes must be list\")\n    for p in passes:\n        if not isinstance(p, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each pass must be dict\")\n        for k in ('name','old','new'):\n            if k not in p:\n                raise ValueError(f\"pass missing {k}\")\n            if not isinstance(p[k], str):\n                raise ValueError(f\"pass.{k} must be string\")\n    max_rounds = case['max_rounds']\n    if not isinstance(max_rounds, int) or max_rounds < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"max_rounds must be non-negative integer\")\n    \n    artifact = text\n    applied = []\n    # handle max_rounds==0 as immediate termination\n    if max_rounds == 0:\n        return {\"artifact\": artifact, \"applied_passes\": applied, \"fixed_point\": True}\n    \n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed_this_sweep = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            if old in artifact:\n                artifact = artifact.replace(old, new, 1)\n                applied.append(p['name'])\n                changed_this_sweep = True\n        if not changed_this_sweep:\n            return {\"artifact\": artifact, \"applied_passes\": applied, \"fixed_point\": True}\n    return {\"artifact\": artifact, \"applied_passes\": applied, \"fixed_point\": False}\n```\n\nThat's the core. Now we need to output as JSON. We'll build a Python dict with key \"implementation\" and value the source code string. Then use json.dumps to serialize. In the response, we output that JSON string.\n\nBut note: The source code itself contains backslashes in the replace call? No, it's fine. Also contains newlines. When we embed in JSON, newlines become \\n, quotes become \\\", etc.\n\nWe'll do something like:\n\nimport json\nsource = \"\"\"...\"\"\" # triple-quoted string\noutput = json.dumps({\"implementation\": source})\nprint(output)\n\nBut we must not import json or do anything in the response; we are just constructing the final JSON object as text.\n\nSo I'll manually escape the source to be safe, but better to compute using json.dumps conceptually. Since we are writing the output directly, we can write the JSON string with proper escaping.\n\nLet me produce the source string with newlines escaped as \\n and quotes as \\\". I'll do that manually.\n\nThe source code above has multiple lines. I'll represent it as a single line with \\n inserted at each line break. Also indent spaces become part of the string.\n\nI'll write:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    # validate\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"case must be dict\\\")\\n    for key in ('text','passes','max_rounds'):\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"missing key {key}\\\")\\n    text = case['text']\\n