present a literal backslash followed by a double quote, we need to write \\\\\\\". Wait, careful.\n\nWhen constructing a JSON string, we need to escape backslashes and double quotes. In the source code, we have literal backslash-n for newlines. That's fine. But for the f-strings, we have `f\"...\"` which in the source code uses double quotes. To include a double quote character inside a JSON string, we write \\\". But in the source code, we have double quotes. So when we embed the source code as a JSON string, we need to replace each double quote with \\\". That is standard.\n\nHowever, the source code also contains backslashes? Actually, the f-strings themselves do not contain backslashes. The only backslashes are the ones we add for newlines. So we don't have to escape additional backslashes.\n\nBut consider the line: `raise ValueError(f\\\"missing key {key}\\\")` - in the source code, that's written as `raise ValueError(f\"missing key {key}\")`. In the JSON string, we need to represent that as `raise ValueError(f\\\"missing key {key}\\\")` but note that the `\\\"` in the JSON string is an escape for double quote. So the actual characters stored will be `raise ValueError(f\"missing key {key}\")` which is correct. However, the backslash in `\\\"` is part of JSON syntax, not part of the stored string. So the stored string will have the double quote, not backslash. That's fine.\n\nBut there is a potential issue: In the source code we have `f\"missing key {key}\"` - this is a valid Python f-string. When we put it inside a JSON string, we need to escape the double quotes. So we write `f\\\"missing key {key}\\\"`. In the JSON text, it will look like `f\\\"missing key {key}\\\"`. When JSON parser reads it, it will interpret `\\\"` as a double quote, so the resulting string will contain `f\"missing key {key}\"` which is correct.\n\nNow what about the outer quotes of the JSON string itself? The JSON string is delimited by double quotes. So we have something like: \"impleme